---
<Layout title="Getting started — dira" description="Install dira, record your first decision, connect Claude Code, and check a plan. A practical guide to decision memory in Git.">
<main id="main" class="container">
<section class="page-heading"><p class="section-id">Guide / From the first decision onward</p><h1>Give your next session<br />a memory of why.</h1><p class="intro">Start with one real choice. Keep the reason and the rejected alternative. Then let the next session pick up where you left off.</p></section>
<div class="guide-layout">
<nav class="guide-nav" aria-label="Guide sections"><p class="section-id">On this page</p>{sections.map(([id,title]) => <a href={`#${id}`}>{title}</a>)}</nav>
<div class="guide-content prose">
<section id="install"><h2>Install dira</h2><p>Homebrew is the quickest path on Apple Silicon Macs, Intel Macs, and Linux AMD64.</p><Code text={'brew install kazi-org/tap/dira\ndira --version'} /><p>For Linux ARM64, or to install without Homebrew, download the matching archive from <a href="https://github.com/kazi-org/dira/releases/latest">GitHub Releases</a>. Each release includes macOS and Linux builds for ARM64 and AMD64, plus a <code>checksums.txt</code> file.</p><h3>Build from source</h3><p>Use the Go toolchain specified in <a href="https://github.com/kazi-org/dira/blob/main/go.mod">go.mod</a>.</p><Code text={'git clone https://github.com/kazi-org/dira.git\ncd dira\ngo build ./cmd/dira\n./dira --help'} /><p>A plain source build reports <code>dev</code>. Release binaries carry their release version. Put the binary on your <code>PATH</code> before configuring hooks.</p></section>
<section id="first-decision"><h2>Record your first decision</h2><p>In your project directory, seed a ledger with a short interview. Choose <code>workspace</code>, then supply an intent, a constraint, and an open question. The interview writes all answers together; an incomplete interview leaves no partial ledger.</p><Code text="dira init --interview" /><p>Record a choice, the option you rejected, and the reason. This example creates <code>dec-0001</code> in a fresh ledger:</p><Code text={log} /><p>Now retrieve it and try a plan that repeats the rejected choice.</p><Code text={'dira why SQLite\ndira check "use PostgreSQL for local storage"'} /><pre class="code"><code>{`✗ conflicts with dec-0001
    rejected alternative: "PostgreSQL"
    why_not: Local storage must work offline without a database server
    revisit_if: Multiple users need concurrent remote writes
→ supersede dec-0001, or revise the plan`}</code></pre><p>Output above is abridged. <code>check</code> exits with <code>2</code> for the conflict. Commit <code>.dira/</code> alongside your code; its cache is derived data.</p><p class="note">Matching is lexical. A result with no conflict means the matcher found none; it is not a proof that a plan has no semantic contradictions.</p></section>
<section id="capture"><h2>Connect your coding agent</h2><p>The bundled integration targets Claude Code. Install its hooks and capture skill once:</p><Code text={'dira install-hooks\ndira install-skill'} /><p>The hooks merge into <code>~/.claude/settings.json</code>, preserving existing registrations. The skill is written under <code>~/.claude/skills/dira/</code>. Start a new session in the repository to receive the brief.</p><div class="table-wrap"><table><thead><tr><th>Event</th><th>What happens</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>SessionStart</td><td><code>brief --context --chain</code> supplies decision context.</td></tr><tr><td>Stop</td><td><code>sniff --stage --quiet</code> stages candidate decisions.</td></tr><tr><td>PreCompact</td><td><code>sniff --deep --stage --all</code> preserves candidates before compaction.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h3>Review is a separate step</h3><Code text="dira distill" /><p>In an interactive terminal, confirm, ignore, edit, or undo a capture. Regex capture never invents a reason or accepts a decision. Confirmation marks a capture for semantic extraction; the entry stays staged until the session agent supplies its reasoning.</p><p>The PreCompact hook captures candidates, but its output alone does not guarantee completed semantic extraction. Check pending captures with <code>distill</code>. The <a href="https://github.com/kazi-org/dira/blob/main/hooks/settings.example.json">hook configuration</a> documents the delivery constraints.</p><p>For another agent, invoke the CLI directly and arrange for <code>dira brief</code> at session start and <code>dira check</code> before planning. The CLI has no model client; semantic work belongs to your agent session.</p></section>
<section id="recall"><h2>Recall the reasoning</h2><Code text={'dira brief\ndira why SQLite\ndira ui'} /><p><code>brief</code> summarizes blockers, active intents, and recent decisions in at most 1,500 tokens. <code>why</code> resolves an entry by ID, title, or tags and follows the reasoning chain. Search by the decision’s title, not just the name of a rejected alternative.</p><p><code>ui</code> serves a read-only browser on localhost. See the same renderer in action in <a href="/why/">Dira’s own ledger</a>.</p><h3>Use check in your workflow</h3><p>Run plans through <code>dira check "your proposed plan"</code> explicitly. The bundled capture hooks do not automatically gate plans.</p><div class="table-wrap"><table><thead><tr><th>check exit code</th><th>Meaning</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><code>0</code></td><td>No conflict detected.</td></tr><tr><td><code>2</code></td><td>A conflict with a settled decision.</td></tr><tr><td><code>1</code></td><td>The check failed to run, including invalid arguments. Never treat this as a clean verdict.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p>Other commands generally use exit <code>2</code> for usage errors. Consult the <a href="/docs/">command reference</a> for each command’s contract.</p></section>
<section id="change"><h2>Change direction without losing history</h2><p>Decisions are allowed to change. First record a replacement with its own reasoning, then supersede the old decision. Use the IDs returned by your ledger:</p><Code text={'dira supersede dec-0001 --with dec-0002 \\\n  --note "Requirements have changed"'} /><p>The replacement gains a <code>supersedes</code> relationship and the old entry becomes superseded. Future checks consult the replacement. Both sides of the change remain in Git.</p></section>
<section id="import"><h2>Bring existing ADRs</h2><Code text="dira import docs/adr" /><p>Dira reports how many documents contain a rejected option with a reason before asking to write. Confirm interactively or pass <code>--yes</code>. Imported decisions are staged and carry the source filename and content hash.</p><p>If the corpus contains no reasoned rejections, dira offers to index it in the cache instead. It does not invent alternatives or turn every document into an enforceable decision.</p></section>
<section id="storage"><h2>Your files are the record</h2><pre class="code"><code>{`.dira/
├── config.toml      # Configuration
├── entries/         # Markdown + YAML frontmatter
│   ├── int-0001.md
│   └── dec-0001.md
└── cache/           # Derived SQLite; rebuildable`}</code></pre><p>There are five entry kinds. Rejected alternatives are part of a decision, rather than a separate kind.</p><div class="table-wrap"><table><thead><tr><th>Kind</th><th>What it records</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Intent</td><td>What you are trying to make true.</td></tr><tr><td>Decision</td><td>A choice, its rejected alternatives, and their reasons.</td></tr><tr><td>Question</td><td>Something unresolved that may block work.</td></tr><tr><td>Constraint</td><td>A rule the work must respect.</td></tr><tr><td>Note</td><td>Supporting context.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p>Relationships connect entries. For example, a decision can <code>derive_from</code> an intent. See the exact edge names and validation rules in the <a href="https://github.com/kazi-org/dira/blob/main/schema/entry.schema.json">entry schema</a>.</p><Code text="dira reindex" /><p>The cache can be rebuilt from the entry files. Read, review, and diff the source with ordinary Git tools.</p></section>
<section id="parents"><h2>Connect context across projects</h2><p>Declare parent ledgers in <code>.dira/config.toml</code> to organize personal, workspace, and repository context. <code>dira brief --chain</code> surfaces parent context; namespaced references connect entries across ledgers.</p><p>Parent context is read at runtime. Follow the <a href="https://github.com/kazi-org/dira/blob/main/docs/design.md">tier and privacy rules</a> when combining private context with public repositories.</p><h3>Direction and execution</h3><p><a href="https://github.com/kazi-org/kazi">kazi</a> checks whether a declared goal satisfies its acceptance predicates. Dira preserves why the goal matters and the choices behind it. Both work independently.</p><Code text="dira map" /><p>The map groups active intents and accepted decisions and reads execution status through kazi’s public JSON interface. If kazi is unavailable, the ledger groups still render with an explanation. Execution status is never stored in the ledger.</p></section>
<section id="troubleshooting"><h2>When something doesn’t look right</h2><h3>The hook cannot find dira</h3><p>Verify <code>dira --version</code> in your shell and ensure the binary is on the <code>PATH</code> available to Claude Code. Start a new session after installing hooks.</p><h3>A capture stays staged</h3><p>Confirmation is not acceptance. The agent still needs to supply semantic reasoning. Review the queue with <code>dira distill</code> and consult the <a href="https://github.com/kazi-org/dira/tree/main/skills/dira">capture skill</a>.</p><h3>why cannot find my search</h3><p>Try the entry’s exact ID, a word from its title, or a tag. The quick-start example uses <code>dira why SQLite</code>.</p><h3>The command list differs</h3><p>This website follows the source tree. Run <code>dira --help</code> for your installed binary and check <a href="https://github.com/kazi-org/dira/releases">release notes</a> before upgrading.</p></section>
<section id="contribute"><h2>Help make the record better</h2><p>Dira is open source under the <a href="https://github.com/kazi-org/dira/blob/main/LICENSE">Apache License 2.0</a>. Read the <a href="https://github.com/kazi-org/dira/blob/main/docs/design.md">design document</a>, explore the <a href="/why/">project’s decisions</a>, or report a reproducible issue on <a href="https://github.com/kazi-org/dira/issues">GitHub</a>.</p><Code text={'sh hooks/install.sh\ngo test ./...'} /><p>Run these from the source checkout before contributing. The tests include validation of Dira’s own ledger against its schema.</p></section>
</div></div></main>
</Layout>
